{"title":"2025 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 25","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem_25\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 25</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Diagram_for_Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Diagram for Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_1_(Cheese,_draw_the_path)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1 (Cheese, draw the path)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(Trivial_Algebra)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (Trivial Algebra)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Upgraded_Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Upgraded Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_FormulaFocus_(Logical,_Good_Editing,_~1:25)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by FormulaFocus (Logical, Good Editing, ~1:25)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution(Quick_and_Simple)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution(Quick and Simple)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_1_by_OmegaLearn.org\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 1 by OmegaLearn.org</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_2_(Fast_and_Easy)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 2 (Fast and Easy)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_3_by_SpreadTheMathLove\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 3 by SpreadTheMathLove</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-11\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_4_by_MadMath\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">11</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 4 by MadMath</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-12\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">12</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_25\">Problem 25</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 25\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Square <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/e/f9efaf9474c5658c4089523e2aff4e11488f8603.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" /> has sides of length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/6/9866e3a998d628ba0941eb4fea0666ac391d149a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"16\" height=\"18\" /> lie on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/b/cfbb4c52e011ceed6e813118a7d59e931af444a2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{AD}$\" width=\"29\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/0/ce0bfc603f9a044e308b1a1fdadca917e81ad2dd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{CD}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"18\" />, respectively, with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/6/ae65c6c8943d07a43ab03a466efe908eb759cfb6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AP=\\frac{8}{5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"67\" height=\"38\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/7/b571dd1de2a18da65aa2f30606b0276feef227e1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$DQ=\\frac{10}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"78\" height=\"38\" />. A path begins along the segment from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/6/9866e3a998d628ba0941eb4fea0666ac391d149a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"16\" height=\"18\" /> and continues by reflecting against the sides of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/e/f9efaf9474c5658c4089523e2aff4e11488f8603.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" /> (with congruent incoming and outgoing angles). If the path hits a vertex of the square, it terminates there; otherwise it continues forever. At which vertex does the path terminate?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/2/fc2a97046f56f475b9dbc35ed58049bd41666b3d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] size(8cm); defaultpen(linewidth(0.9)); dotfactor = 3; pair A=(0,0), B=(4,0), C=(4,4), D=(0,4); pair P=(0, 8/5);           pair Q=(10/3, 4);      draw(A--B--C--D--cycle); draw(P--Q, red+linewidth(1.2)); label(&quot;$A$&quot;,A,SW); label(&quot;$B$&quot;,B,SE); label(&quot;$C$&quot;,C,NE); label(&quot;$D$&quot;,D,NW); label(&quot;$P$&quot;,P,W); label(&quot;$Q$&quot;,Q,N); draw(arc(Q,0.3,0, -35.78)); draw(arc(Q,0.3,180,180+35.78));  pair arrow_dir = Q + 0.75*dir(-35.78); draw(Q--arrow_dir, red+linewidth(1.2), EndArrow(size=10, FillDraw(red)));  dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D); dot(P); dot(Q); [/asy]\" width=\"378\" height=\"378\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/c/3bc686c543ada9b28d5ca6c94af203e22acbf800.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) } A \\qquad \\textbf{(B) } B \\qquad \\textbf{(C) } C \\qquad \\textbf{(D) } D  \\qquad \\textbf{(E) } \\text{The path continues forever.}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"568\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Diagram_for_Solution_2\">Diagram for Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Diagram for Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-center\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Amc10b2025q25.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/9/9d/Amc10b2025q25.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"650\" height=\"467\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_1_.28Cheese.2C_draw_the_path.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1_(Cheese,_draw_the_path)\">Solution 1 (Cheese, draw the path)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1 (Cheese, draw the path)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>For this question, I cheesed it by just drawing the path with a ruler and an compass, and you can find that the path eventually terminates at vertex B by doing so. Therefore, the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/b/3bb156e3426968250c914b6c0c04905ea70e860c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\mathbf{(B)\\,\\mathnormal{B}}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"60\" height=\"32\" />.\n</p><p>~EZ123\n</p><p>Note - Protractors are not allowed on the AMC Exam, but you can sort of achieve the same thing with a compass\n</p><p>Remark: I actually did this on the test, and I didn't even sketch out the diagram either. I had like a minute left on the clock, and I traced the path of the line lightly on my screen (I was taking digital version) and somehow got it correctly lol. I didn't even use a compass or anything either 🤣 (verified tracing, works. You don't even have to be very accurate to see that the answer is B- Happymaths)\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28Trivial_Algebra.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(Trivial_Algebra)\">Solution 2 (Trivial Algebra)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (Trivial Algebra)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>First, let me introduce the reflection trick.  Basically, instead of bending the line segment a bunch of times, map any path of the line segment into a reflected square, reflecting the square about the segment that the ray touches.  Every time this happens, we map the latest square onto a new one.  After we do this many times, the entire path becomes a straight line:\n</p><p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://ibb.co/VkCbKNT\">Illustration link, hosted on imgbb (I'm too tired to use Asymptote)</a>\n</p><p>So now, we could just imagine the whole reflection path as a huge straight ray in the coordinate plane.  When it lands upon a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> both divisible by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, since a corner reflected is still a corner, we can then trace the corner back.\n</p><p>Note that by some trivial arithmetic you get that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/2/922a1922d27edefdeed49f01822f51e4ba7e9d72.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PD=4-\\frac{8}{5}=\\frac{12}{5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"147\" height=\"38\" />, and if we let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> be the origin of a coordinate plane, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/5/bc56862b1fdb71204894da278e42ef75de73a40b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> be the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />-axis and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> be the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />-axis, the slope of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/b/7ab17967324fc2b12349c0610c6c256f4c829162.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PQ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"30\" height=\"18\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/8/5/885450d131eac822dc6e967ecf8f6fea444842e5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{12}{5}\\div\\frac{10}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"68\" height=\"38\" /> using rise over run, which is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/c/37c5bc4d22037ea3a670643a26e2befbe1e4f9b4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{18}{25}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"24\" height=\"38\" />.  Trivially, the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />-intercept of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/b/7ab17967324fc2b12349c0610c6c256f4c829162.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PQ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"30\" height=\"18\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/8/778abab8ebf560b512a9a7e95a1c404065dc9cb0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{8}{5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"37\" />, so the equation of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/b/7ab17967324fc2b12349c0610c6c256f4c829162.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PQ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"30\" height=\"18\" /> becomes\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/6/74637dd4d1dd60e31bb7adef6f775cea9c864067.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[y=\\frac{18}{25}x+\\frac{8}{5}\\]\" width=\"99\" height=\"37\" />\nand you want both <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> to be divisible by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />.  Right off the bat, note that the denominator in the linear term is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/6/66644fd9f411299c3c1eabf151b5f601c9ef6c21.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$25$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />, so to have any chance of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> being an integer AT ALL, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> must be divisible by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> (else the resulting fraction will have something over <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/6/66644fd9f411299c3c1eabf151b5f601c9ef6c21.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$25$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> plus something over <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, so there's extra <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/a/a6ad35b9fd964dab4022f116eb90a00220c45044.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1/25$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"38\" height=\"20\" />s.)\n</p><p>Therefore, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> must be a multiple of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/3/f8366cd6196dd8a9da6d38a3e9eafb109e99d53e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$20$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />.  Then let's try <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/c/f/1cf1342f460285dfd226094ac3973558576c9982.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=20$\" width=\"52\" height=\"12\" />!  We get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/7/bf709e521fe3f0bae584d735bda8a7c0b5627984.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y=16$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"51\" height=\"16\" />, which is divisible by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />!  Yay!!!  You might start to sweat about the exact path and how to trace the vertices now, but that does not matter: we can see that if we first reflect to the right and then up, it's no difference than if we went up and then right.  So it doesn't matter if we do all the horizontal or vertical reflections all together.\n</p><p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://ibb.co/fYJc6Ks1\">Illustration link, hosted on imgbb (I'm too tired to use Asymptote)</a>\n</p><p>Now you can just label a random square <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/e/f9efaf9474c5658c4089523e2aff4e11488f8603.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" />, and reflect to the right <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> times (since you already have 1 square) and then up <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> times.  You'll then notice that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/7/897037dbcf303e2491288c44d549a94d02e53ea7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(20,16)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"60\" height=\"20\" /> is just a copy of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/b/3bb156e3426968250c914b6c0c04905ea70e860c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\mathbf{(B)\\,\\mathnormal{B}}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"60\" height=\"32\" />.\n</p><p>~johnzhou721\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Upgraded_Solution_1\">Upgraded Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Upgraded Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Use the scratch paper's corner and fold it to necessary to mold and replicate angles, then use a straightedge to trace it.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_by_FormulaFocus_.28Logical.2C_Good_Editing.2C_.7E1:25.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_FormulaFocus_(Logical,_Good_Editing,_~1:25)\">Video Solution by FormulaFocus (Logical, Good Editing, ~1:25)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by FormulaFocus (Logical, Good Editing, ~1:25)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/shorts/OpoccysZv3s\">https://www.youtube.com/shorts/OpoccysZv3s</a>\n</p><p>~Ebai\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution.28Quick_and_Simple.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution(Quick_and_Simple)\">Video Solution(Quick and Simple)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution(Quick and Simple)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Hr9eqMTnx8g\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Hr9eqMTnx8g</a>\n~Sigma_Bob\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_1_by_OmegaLearn.org\">Video Solution 1 by OmegaLearn.org</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 1 by OmegaLearn.org\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/honKBgPkly4\">https://youtu.be/honKBgPkly4</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_2_.28Fast_and_Easy.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_2_(Fast_and_Easy)\">Video Solution 2 (Fast and Easy)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 2 (Fast and Easy)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=v8eH8myCSxo\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=v8eH8myCSxo</a>\n</p><p>~ Pi Academy\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_3_by_SpreadTheMathLove\">Video Solution 3 by SpreadTheMathLove</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 3 by SpreadTheMathLove\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-ZOTMCwLXGE\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-ZOTMCwLXGE<